const BATCH_FORMATS = COMMON + `
YOUR BATCH: the set-hook -B monitor subsystem and the display-message -v format trace: two groups, 3 items, the last two format-owned groups in the registry. WORKDIR: zz-opus-termopts (warm zz-mux/zz-terminal build; it exists here and is clean at a cycle-10 tip). BRANCHNAME: batch-formats-monitors-trace-opus.
Your zones: crates/zz-mux/src/formats.rs, status.rs, command.rs, tmux_options.rs, honest_knobs.rs and parser.rs; crates/zz-protocol/src/catalog.rs (the display-message -v promotion); crates/zz-daemon/src/status.rs. Allowed excursion, minimal and listed one by one in notes: crates/zz-daemon/src/daemon.rs ONLY for the monitor tick and the hook-firing path it calls. The other worker owns crates/zz-protocol/src/key.rs, message.rs, lib.rs and snapshot.rs, crates/zz-terminal, crates/zz-tui, crates/zz-ui, crates/zz/src, crates/zz-client, crates/zz-mux/src/model.rs, layout.rs and sort.rs, and daemon.rs's chooser, overlay-prompt and menu regions plus crates/zz-daemon/src/keys.rs. Keep your daemon.rs hunks away from those regions.
PROTOCOL: the OTHER lane owns the 96 -> 97 bump this cycle; you do NOT bump. Nothing in your batch should be wire-reachable (monitors fire through the existing hook path, -v prints through the existing command output). If you find a wire change you cannot avoid, stop that piece, record it in notes, and implement the rest.

Groups in order:
1. formats.context-producer-fidelity (1: semantic:format-context-producer-fidelity). HARD BUDGET 100 MINUTES. The nine remaining literal tuples are all notify_monitor_cb and the group reason carries the whole subsystem measured twice; re-measure the two parse failures and the fire shape yourself, then build. The pin: monitor.c is 658 lines plus notify.c glue. monitor_parse splits the -B value on the first TWO colons into name, what and format and fails without both; what is '%*' for all panes, '%N' for one pane, '@*' for all windows, '@N' for one window, and anything else INCLUDING the empty string for the session; cmd_set_option then refuses a name that does not start with '@' ("monitor hook name must start with @", status 1) and a bad value answers "invalid subscription: <value>" at status 1; note that set-hook -Bu @watch does NOT remove a subscription because -B takes a value and swallows the u (it answers "invalid subscription: u"), while set-hook -u -B @watch does. monitor_add arms a ONE-SECOND evtimer that re-expands each subscription's format per subscribed object; monitor_check_value records the FIRST sample as a baseline and calls back only when the string CHANGED, because notify_monitor_add passes no MONITOR_NOTIFY_INITIAL, so nothing fires on arm and nothing fires while the value is unchanged. The hook body is the option's own value, set by the command's positional argument, and notify_insert_hook looks it up on the store the monitor was created against. show-hooks -B lists the live subscriptions back as name:what:format, one per line in NAME order. The fire carries nine names: hook, hook_value, hook_last, hook_window, hook_window_name, hook_window_index, hook_session, hook_session_name, hook_pane, each empty where the scope has no such object.
   Build it as: a monitor store keyed by option scope and name (crates/zz-mux/src/command.rs or the daemon, whichever is smaller), the one-second tick in the daemon, and the nine names as a PRODUCER on the existing hook_format_variables path in command.rs (around line 16282), which already returns a name-to-value map the expander reads as tree entries - a producer, not a format-table entry. zz answers "invalid flag -B" at status 1 from both set-hook and show-hooks today, and that is a mux-side rejection, not a catalog one: -B is ALREADY a real catalog option on both commands (CommandOptionSpec::value("-B", FreeForm, ...) on set-hook, a flag on show-hooks), so no catalog counter or usage override moves with this work. Prove on both binaries with 'set -g @fired' bodies, never run-shell (a run-shell body is why the first attempt saw nothing fire): a window monitor '@watch:@*:#{window_name}' fires nothing on arm, nothing while unchanged, and once after rename-window renamed with hook=@watch, hook_value=renamed, hook_last=<old name>, hook_window=@0, hook_window_name=renamed, hook_window_index=0, hook_session=$0, hook_session_name=<name> and an empty hook_pane; a session monitor '@sess::#{session_name}' after rename-session; a pane monitor '@panew:%*:#{pane_title}' after select-pane -T; show-hooks -B ordering; both parse failures; the -u removal and the -Bu non-removal. The compat_manifest_tests producer partition moves with the close, so recount it and run cargo test -p zz-mux. Scenario: compat/scenarios/smoke/format-monitor-hooks.txt with a fixture (the one-second tick means the fixture must wait for the tick, not poll instantly).
2. display-message.verbose-trace (2: flag:display-message:-v, semantic:display-message-format-trace). HARD BUDGET 100 MINUTES, and it is the harder of the two: start it only after group 1 is committed and its proofs re-run. The group reason carries the full line grammar and both structural mismatches. The trace goes to the command's stdout AHEAD of the result through cmdq_print and is emitted whether or not -p was given, because format_log1 gates only on ft->item != NULL and FORMAT_VERBOSE. Every line is '#', then es->loop spaces, then the message (format_log1 writes "#%.*s%s" with es->loop indexing a ten-space literal); format_expand1 increments es->loop on entry, so a top-level expansion logs at ONE space and each nested expansion adds one. Mapped onto zz's expander, whose top call is expand(format, 0): expand logs at depth + 1 and expand_replacement, called with depth + 1, logs at its own depth, so the two agree by construction. The measured corpus for display-message -v -p '#{?pane_dead,dead,#{=3:pane_title}}' on a pane titled macbook is in the reason in order, ending 'replaced ...' / 'result is: mac'; reproduce it exactly on the pin first, then match it. About 45 distinct messages exist in format.c (the expand frame, the finds, the modifier pair, the lookup pair, the conditional six, the comparison three, the post-transforms, the tail, the loop family, the expression family of seven, the literal string, the two content-search lines, the repeat syntax error and the time limit): implement the ones zz's expander can reach and write down which pin lines cannot occur in zz and why.
   THE FIRST STRUCTURAL MISMATCH IS REAL WORK, NOT LOGGING: the pin expands each modifier ARGUMENT through format_expand1 WHILE it parses the modifiers (that is where the 'expanding format: 3' / 'result is: 3' pair at depth three comes from) and logs the modifier only after the whole list is parsed, while zz's build_modifiers collects arguments as raw slices and expands them later and only where needed. Moving the expansion into parsing is a behaviour change and it is observable: with a user option @three=3 the pin answers '#{=#{@three}:pane_title}' as the LITERAL '=3:pane_title' (the modifier parse fails on a non-numeric argument and the body is then expanded as a plain format) and '#{s/#{@three}/X/:pane_title}' as the untouched title, where zz answers the plain title for both. Measure both on the pin, match them, and do NOT break '#{I/c:X}', whose body is never expanded (the I modifier landed in cycle 10). The SECOND mismatch has no site: zz has no counterpart for format_check_time or the strftime overflow, so 'reached time limit' and 'format is too long' cannot occur; record that rather than faking it. Then promote -v in catalog.rs (counters and usage overrides move; recount compat_manifest_tests.rs) and prove the corpus differentially on both binaries with the SAME final expansion as the ordinary path, over the acceptance's corpus: a plain lookup, a missing name, a nested conditional, a modifier with arguments, a literal, the length and padding limits, a comparison, a substitution, and a style body. Scenario: compat/scenarios/smoke/format-trace.txt.
Scenario names as listed per group; add summary rows so --check-summary stays green. If group 2 runs out of budget, land group 1 plus whatever of the trace is committed and green, and write the remaining line families into the reason.`

